---
title: Corpus story lab
teaser: "Several different stories hiding in the same writing archive."
layout: layouts/base.njk
permalink: /stats/story-lab/
noindex: true
eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true
---
<section class="kh-story-lab">
  <!--
    STORY LAB ASSEMBLY NOTES

    This page is a build-time view of the writing corpus, not a client-side
    dashboard. Its main input is src/site/_data/storyLab.json, generated by
    scripts/compute-story-lab.mjs. That generator combines four sources:

    1. Post frontmatter for dates, primary topics, series, tags and content type.
    2. semanticMap.json for whole-entry semantic neighbours.
    3. build/semantic-search/vectors.json for passage-level comparisons.
    4. Rendered article HTML in build/ for authored internal links and outbound hosts.

    The interactive script only adds coordinated highlighting and detail text.
    Every chart is rendered as meaningful HTML or SVG before JavaScript runs.

    The production build first renders bootstrap HTML, regenerates embeddings
    and all three ignored semantic data files, then renders the final site.
    Adding or editing a post therefore requires only `yarn build`; none of the
    generated JSON belongs in version control.

    Most experiments are generated from rules and will absorb new entries.
    Editorial exceptions and fixed thresholds are documented beside each view
    below. Longer methodology notes live in docs/CORPUS_STORY_LAB.md.
  -->
  <h1 class="kh-font-headline kh-font-headline--display kh-text-heading--1">{{ title }}</h1>

  <div class="kh-content">
    <p><strong>An experimental playground, not a proposed page.</strong> Each view
    starts with a different question. The semantic model suggests which texts
    resemble one another, although many were already deliberately cross-linked;
    the interpretation still belongs to the writer.<sup><a class="kh-lab-citation" href="#source-pudding" aria-label="Source 10: The Pudding's guide to visual storytelling">[10]</a></sup></p>
  </div>

  <nav class="kh-lab-index" aria-label="Story experiments">
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#returns">Ideas that came back</a></li>
      <li><a href="#biographies">Biographies of a few ideas</a></li>
      <li><a href="#practice">From argument to evidence</a></li>
      <li><a href="#career">Themes read against career chapters</a></li>
      <li><a href="#pulses">When themes became active</a></li>
      <li><a href="#diagnostic">Is this really a long-term network?</a></li>
      <li><a href="#outbound">The web behind the writing</a></li>
      <li><a href="#passages">Passages the map averaged away</a></li>
      <li><a href="#radial">Topic families and two kinds of connection</a></li>
      <li><a href="#sparks">When subjects entered the archive</a></li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <!--
    1. IDEAS THAT CAME BACK
    Automatic: uses every semantic-map pair at least three years apart, excluding
    companion pieces such as the same series or article and impact-story pairs.
    Editorial rule: the three-year floor defines what this experiment calls a
    return. The four text rows below the chart are simply the four longest gaps.
  -->
  <div class="kh-section-head kh-u-margin__top--1200">
    <h2 id="returns" class="kh-text-heading--2"><span class="kh-section-number">1.0</span>Ideas that came back</h2>
    <span class="kh-meta">{{ storyLab.returnEdges | length }} echoes after 3+ years</span>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-content">
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> which ideas followed me between jobs, technologies
    and years? Time finally earns the horizontal axis. Only non-companion pairs
    separated by at least three years are shown, so an arc is a return rather
    than two versions of the same recent piece. Some are deliberate callbacks;
    others are uncited echoes.<sup><a class="kh-lab-citation" href="#source-shape-of-song" aria-label="Source 1: The Shape of Song">[1]</a></sup></p>
  </div>
  <figure class="kh-lab-figure">
    <div class="kh-map-scroll">
      <svg class="kh-map kh-lab-arc" viewBox="0 0 1200 320" width="1200" height="320" role="img" aria-labelledby="returns-title returns-desc">
        <title id="returns-title">Long-distance semantic echoes through the writing archive</title>
        <desc id="returns-desc">Entries run chronologically from 2013 to 2026. Curves connect nine related pairs separated by at least three years. Career chapters appear beneath the baseline as context, not causes.</desc>
        <g class="kh-lab-arc__careers" aria-hidden="true">
          {%- for career in storyLab.careerBands %}
          <rect x="{{ career.x }}" y="260" width="{{ career.width }}" height="34"></rect>
          <text x="{{ career.x + 8 }}" y="282">{{ career.label }}</text>
          {%- endfor %}
        </g>
        <g class="kh-lab-arc__links">
          {%- for edge in storyLab.returnEdges %}
          <path d="{{ edge.path }}">
            <title>{{ edge.first.title }} to {{ edge.second.title }}, {{ edge.gapYears }} years, similarity {{ edge.score }}</title>
          </path>
          {%- endfor %}
        </g>
        <line class="kh-lab-axis" x1="72" y1="250" x2="1128" y2="250"></line>
        <g class="kh-lab-arc__entries">
          {%- for node in storyLab.nodes %}
          <a href="{{ node.url }}"><title>{{ node.date }} — {{ node.title }}</title><line x1="{{ node.x }}" y1="245" x2="{{ node.x }}" y2="255"></line></a>
          {%- endfor %}
        </g>
        <g class="kh-lab-years" aria-hidden="true">
          {%- for year in [2013, 2015, 2017, 2019, 2021, 2023, 2025] %}
          <text x="{{ 72 + ((year - 2011) / 16 * 1056) }}" y="314">{{ year }}</text>
          {%- endfor %}
        </g>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <figcaption class="kh-meta">Career bands locate the writing in time; they do not claim that an employer caused a theme.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <ol class="kh-return-list">
    {%- for edge in storyLab.featuredReturns %}
    <li class="kh-return">
      <a href="{{ edge.first.url }}">{{ edge.first.title }}</a>
      <span class="kh-return__hop" style="--kh-from: {{ edge.fromPercent }}%; --kh-to: {{ edge.toPercent }}%" aria-hidden="true"><i></i></span>
      <a href="{{ edge.second.url }}">{{ edge.second.title }}</a>
      <span class="kh-meta">{{ edge.first.year }} → {{ edge.second.year }} · {{ edge.gapYears }} years</span>
    </li>
    {%- endfor %}
  </ol>

  <!--
    2. BIOGRAPHIES OF A FEW IDEAS
    Hybrid: scripts/compute-story-lab.mjs names each lens and supplies two to
    four anchor entries. It averages their non-header passage embeddings, ranks
    the corpus against that centroid, applies a labelled similarity floor, and
    limits entries from any one year. New posts can therefore join automatically
    without pretending that the model named or interpreted the biography.
  -->
  <div class="kh-section-head kh-u-margin__top--1200">
    <h2 id="biographies" class="kh-text-heading--2"><span class="kh-section-number">2.0</span>Biographies of a few ideas</h2>
    <span class="kh-meta">Semantically assembled trails</span>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-content">
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> how did a concern change as it moved from a small
    technical problem to an organizational argument? Each broad lens starts with
    a few editorial anchors, then passage similarity chooses the entries. A
    per-year cap keeps a recent publishing burst from swallowing the history;
    the names and interpretation remain editorial.<sup><a class="kh-lab-citation" href="#source-dear-data" aria-label="Source 7: Dear Data">[7]</a></sup><sup><a class="kh-lab-citation" href="#source-writing-without-words" aria-label="Source 8: Writing Without Words">[8]</a></sup></p>
  </div>
  <ul class="kh-lab-key" aria-label="Entry types">
    <li><i class="kh-lab-key__mark kh-lab-key__mark--article" aria-hidden="true"></i>Writing</li>
    <li><i class="kh-lab-key__mark kh-lab-key__mark--impact" aria-hidden="true"></i>Impact story</li>
    <li><i class="kh-lab-key__mark kh-lab-key__mark--digesting" aria-hidden="true"></i>Digesting note</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="kh-trails">
    {%- for trail in storyLab.trails %}
    <section class="kh-trail" aria-labelledby="trail-{{ loop.index }}">
      <h3 id="trail-{{ loop.index }}" class="kh-text-heading--3">{{ trail.name }}</h3>
      <p class="kh-trail__description kh-meta">{{ trail.description }} Similarity ≥ {{ trail.minScore }}.</p>
      <div class="kh-trail__plot" aria-hidden="true">
        <i class="kh-trail__line" style="--kh-from: {{ trail.nodes[0].xPercent }}%; --kh-to: {{ trail.nodes[trail.nodes.length - 1].xPercent }}%"></i>
        {%- for node in trail.nodes %}<i class="kh-trail__node kh-trail__node--{{ node.kind }}" style="--kh-at: {{ node.xPercent }}%"></i>{%- endfor %}
      </div>
      <ol class="kh-trail__entries">
        {%- for node in trail.nodes %}<li><span class="kh-meta">{{ node.year }}</span> <a href="{{ node.url }}">{{ node.title }}</a>{% if node.similarity %} <span class="kh-trail__score kh-meta" title="Cosine similarity to the editorial anchor centroid">{{ node.similarity }}</span>{% endif %}</li>{%- endfor %}
      </ol>
    </section>
    {%- endfor %}
  </div>

  <!--
    3. FROM ARGUMENT TO EVIDENCE
    Automatic: filters semantic-map pairs so exactly one entry is an impact story
    and the other is not. Content type comes from tags and filename conventions.
    The view therefore updates when a new impact story enters the semantic map.
  -->
  <div class="kh-section-head kh-u-margin__top--1200">
    <h2 id="practice" class="kh-text-heading--2"><span class="kh-section-number">3.0</span>From argument to evidence</h2>
    <span class="kh-meta">{{ storyLab.practiceEdges | length }} article ↔ impact-story pairs</span>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-content">
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> where does the blog turn practice into an argument,
    and where does the work section give that argument receipts? These are often
    two accounts of the same work, so the interest is in genre and perspective,
    not independent discovery.</p>
  </div>
  <figure class="kh-lab-figure">
    <div class="kh-map-scroll">
      <svg class="kh-map kh-lab-practice" viewBox="0 0 1200 250" width="1200" height="250" role="img" aria-labelledby="practice-title practice-desc">
        <title id="practice-title">Connections between articles and impact stories</title>
        <desc id="practice-desc">Articles run chronologically along the upper line and impact stories along the lower line. Curves join semantically related entries written in the two genres.</desc>
        <text class="kh-lab-lane-label" x="18" y="82">writing</text>
        <text class="kh-lab-lane-label" x="18" y="196">work</text>
        <line class="kh-lab-axis" x1="72" y1="78" x2="1128" y2="78"></line>
        <line class="kh-lab-axis" x1="72" y1="192" x2="1128" y2="192"></line>
        <g class="kh-lab-practice__links">
          {%- for edge in storyLab.practiceEdges %}<path d="{{ edge.path }}"><title>{{ edge.article.title }} ↔ {{ edge.impact.title }}, similarity {{ edge.score }}</title></path>{%- endfor %}
        </g>
        <g class="kh-lab-practice__nodes">
          {%- for edge in storyLab.practiceEdges %}
          <a href="{{ edge.article.url }}"><circle cx="{{ edge.article.x }}" cy="78" r="4"><title>{{ edge.article.title }}</title></circle></a>
          <a href="{{ edge.impact.url }}"><rect x="{{ edge.impact.x - 4 }}" y="188" width="8" height="8" transform="rotate(45 {{ edge.impact.x }} 192)"><title>{{ edge.impact.title }}</title></rect></a>
          {%- endfor %}
        </g>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <figcaption class="kh-meta">Circles are articles; diamonds are impact stories. Repeated marks show that one entry can be paired with several accounts.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <!--
    4. THEMES READ AGAINST CAREER CHAPTERS
    Automatic counts: the ten most frequent primary topics are recalculated, and
    cells count posts within each career date band. Fill uses a shared square-root
    count scale; printed percentages are normalized within a chapter.
    Bespoke context: employer labels and their start and end dates are maintained
    in the careers array in scripts/compute-story-lab.mjs.
  -->
  <div class="kh-section-head kh-u-margin__top--1200">
    <h2 id="career" class="kh-text-heading--2"><span class="kh-section-number">4.0</span>Themes read against career chapters</h2>
    <span class="kh-meta">Color: post count · percentage: chapter share</span>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-content">
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> which subjects appear in the public writing from
    each chapter? Darker cells mean more posts on a shared scale, so four posts
    always read as more than two. The percentage says how prominent that topic
    was within its chapter. This is context, not a claim of correlation or
    organizational influence.<sup><a class="kh-lab-citation" href="#source-themeriver" aria-label="Source 2: ThemeRiver">[2]</a></sup></p>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-table-scroll">
    <table class="kh-table--spec kh-lab-heatmap">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Primary topic</th>{% for career in storyLab.careerBands %}<th scope="col">{{ career.label }} <span class="kh-spark__axis">{{ career.tagged }} tagged</span></th>{% endfor %}</tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {%- for row in storyLab.topicCareer %}
        <tr><th scope="row">{{ row.topic }} <span class="kh-meta">{{ row.total }}</span></th>
          {%- for cell in row.cells %}<td style="--kh-strength: {{ cell.strength }}%"><span>{{ cell.count }}</span><small>{{ cell.share }}%</small></td>{%- endfor %}
        </tr>
        {%- endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <!--
    5. WHEN THEMES BECAME ACTIVE
    Automatic: uses the same recalculated top ten primary topics as section 4 and
    counts them by publication year. Each row is normalized to its own peak, so
    color compares a topic with itself rather than comparing volumes across rows.
    The displayed year array is currently fixed at 2013 through 2026.
  -->
  <div class="kh-section-head kh-u-margin__top--1200">
    <h2 id="pulses" class="kh-text-heading--2"><span class="kh-section-number">5.0</span>When themes became active</h2>
    <span class="kh-meta">Each row scaled to its own peak year</span>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-content">
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> did a theme persist, arrive in a burst, or return
    after an absence? This is closer to a score than a river: every year stays
    aligned, including the quiet ones, and each row states its total.<sup><a class="kh-lab-citation" href="#source-themeriver" aria-label="Source 2: ThemeRiver">[2]</a></sup><sup><a class="kh-lab-citation" href="#source-history-flow" aria-label="Source 5: History Flow">[5]</a></sup></p>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-table-scroll">
    <table class="kh-table--spec kh-lab-pulses">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Topic</th>{% for year in storyLab.years %}<th scope="col">{{ year }}</th>{% endfor %}</tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {%- for row in storyLab.topicYears %}
        <tr><th scope="row">{{ row.topic }} <span class="kh-meta">{{ row.total }}</span></th>
          {%- for cell in row.cells %}<td style="--kh-strength: {{ cell.strength }}%"><span class="sr-only">{{ cell.year }}: {{ cell.count }} entries</span>{% if cell.count %}<b aria-hidden="true">{{ cell.count }}</b>{% endif %}</td>{%- endfor %}
        </tr>
        {%- endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <!--
    6. IS THIS REALLY A LONG-TERM NETWORK?
    Automatic: plots every displayed whole-entry semantic-map edge by elapsed
    years and similarity. New entries affect this only if they survive the public
    map's similarity floor and per-entry neighbour cap. Plot domains are fixed.
  -->
  <div class="kh-section-head kh-u-margin__top--1200">
    <h2 id="diagnostic" class="kh-text-heading--2"><span class="kh-section-number">6.0</span>Is this really a long-term network?</h2>
    <span class="kh-meta">All {{ storyLab.counts.links }} displayed semantic links</span>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-content">
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> how much of the apparent network is recurrence,
    and how much is a recent burst describing the same work? Most links sit near
    zero years. The rare points on the right are the better raw material for a
    story about ideas returning.</p>
  </div>
  <figure class="kh-lab-figure">
    <div class="kh-map-scroll">
      <svg class="kh-map kh-lab-scatter" viewBox="0 0 1200 280" width="1200" height="280" role="img" aria-labelledby="scatter-title scatter-desc">
        <title id="scatter-title">Semantic similarity by years between linked entries</title>
        <desc id="scatter-desc">Most of the sixty-six pairs are less than one year apart. Filled points cross a career boundary; open points do not. Faded points are likely article and impact-story companions.</desc>
        <g class="kh-lab-grid" aria-hidden="true">
          {% for gap in [0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10] %}<line x1="{{ 72 + (gap / 10 * 1040) }}" y1="34" x2="{{ 72 + (gap / 10 * 1040) }}" y2="228"></line><text x="{{ 72 + (gap / 10 * 1040) }}" y="254">{{ gap }}y</text>{% endfor %}
          {% for score in [0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9] %}<line x1="72" y1="{{ 228 - ((score - 0.6) / 0.32 * 190) }}" x2="1112" y2="{{ 228 - ((score - 0.6) / 0.32 * 190) }}"></line><text x="28" y="{{ 232 - ((score - 0.6) / 0.32 * 190) }}">{{ score }}</text>{% endfor %}
        </g>
        <g class="kh-lab-scatter__points">
          {%- for point in storyLab.scatter %}
          <circle cx="{{ point.x }}" cy="{{ point.y }}" r="{% if point.gapYears >= 3 %}5{% else %}3.5{% endif %}" class="{% if point.crossCareer %}is-cross-career {% endif %}{% if point.companion %}is-companion{% endif %}"><title>{{ point.first.title }} + {{ point.second.title }}: {{ point.gapYears }} years, similarity {{ point.score }}</title></circle>
          {%- endfor %}
        </g>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <figcaption class="kh-meta">Horizontal position is elapsed time; vertical position is model similarity. Filled marks cross a career boundary. Faded marks are likely companion pieces.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <!--
    7. THE WEB BEHIND THE WRITING
    Automatic after a rendered build: scans anchors inside each article, ranks
    hosts by distinct entries mentioning them, and counts suffix classes within
    career chapters. The top-fifteen limit and suffix classification rules are
    maintained in scripts/compute-story-lab.mjs. These links imply reference,
    not endorsement, influence or trust.
  -->
  <div class="kh-section-head kh-u-margin__top--1200">
    <h2 id="outbound" class="kh-text-heading--2"><span class="kh-section-number">7.0</span>The web behind the writing</h2>
    <span class="kh-meta">{{ storyLab.outbound.links }} links · {{ storyLab.outbound.uniqueHosts }} hosts</span>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-content">
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> which parts of the wider web does this archive
    repeatedly use as evidence, infrastructure or cultural context? Ranking by
    distinct entries prevents one reference-heavy article from winning merely
    because it links to the same host many times. Raw link counts remain visible.</p>
  </div>
  <figure class="kh-lab-figure">
    <ol class="kh-domain-list">
      {%- for domain in storyLab.outbound.topDomains %}
      <li class="kh-domain">
        <a href="{{ domain.href }}">{{ domain.host }}</a>
        <span class="kh-domain__bar" style="--kh-domain-width: {{ domain.bar }}%" aria-hidden="true"><i></i></span>
        <span class="kh-meta">{{ domain.entries }} entries · {{ domain.links }} links</span>
      </li>
      {%- endfor %}
    </ol>
    <figcaption class="kh-meta">Ranked by the number of entries containing each host. Image credits and project links are included because they are part of the published article.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3 class="kh-text-heading--3">Address endings across career chapters</h3>
  <div class="kh-content">
    <p>Each host counts once per entry. Darker cells mean more entry–host
    mentions on a shared scale; percentages show the mix within that chapter.
    An address ending is not an institutional identity: GitHub is still a
    <code>.com</code>, and <code>.org</code> does not guarantee nonprofit status.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-table-scroll">
    <table class="kh-table--spec kh-lab-suffix">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Address ending</th>{% for career in storyLab.careerBands %}<th scope="col">{{ career.label }}</th>{% endfor %}</tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {%- for row in storyLab.outbound.suffixCareer %}
        <tr><th scope="row">{{ row.label }}</th>
          {%- for cell in row.cells %}<td style="--kh-strength: {{ cell.strength }}%"><span>{{ cell.count }}</span><small>{{ cell.share }}%</small></td>{%- endfor %}
        </tr>
        {%- endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <!--
    8. PASSAGES THE MAP AVERAGED AWAY
    Automatic after embeddings are regenerated: compares non-header passage
    vectors for entry pairs separated by at least three years that did not become
    public semantic-map edges. The passage floor, minimum lift and eight-result
    display limit are exploratory constants in scripts/compute-story-lab.mjs.
  -->
  <div class="kh-section-head kh-u-margin__top--1200">
    <h2 id="passages" class="kh-text-heading--2"><span class="kh-section-number">8.0</span>Passages the map averaged away</h2>
    <span class="kh-meta">{{ storyLab.semanticIndex.entryChunks }} chunks from {{ storyLab.semanticIndex.entryPages }} entries</span>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-content">
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> which small echoes disappear when the semantic
    search index is averaged into one vector per entry? These candidates span at
    least three years, have no displayed map edge, and contain one passage pair
    that is substantially closer than the two entries overall. The snippets are
    prompts for close reading, not proof that the ideas are related.</p>
  </div>
  <ol class="kh-passage-list">
    {%- for echo in storyLab.semanticIndex.passageEchoes %}
    <li class="kh-passage">
      <div class="kh-passage__pair">
        <div>
          <span class="kh-meta">{{ echo.first.year }}</span>
          <a href="{{ echo.first.url }}">{{ echo.first.title }}</a>
          <p>{{ echo.firstSnippet }}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="kh-passage__score">
          <span>{{ echo.passageScore }} passage</span>
          <i aria-hidden="true">↕</i>
          <span>{{ echo.entryScore }} whole entry</span>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span class="kh-meta">{{ echo.second.year }}</span>
          <a href="{{ echo.second.url }}">{{ echo.second.title }}</a>
          <p>{{ echo.secondSnippet }}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <span class="kh-meta">{{ echo.gapYears }} years apart · {% if echo.explicitLink %}linked directly in the writing{% else %}no direct source link found{% endif %}</span>
    </li>
    {%- endfor %}
  </ol>
  <p class="kh-meta">The latest full-build index contains {{ storyLab.semanticIndex.totalChunks }} chunks from {{ storyLab.semanticIndex.totalPages }} searchable pages. The corpus map uses {{ storyLab.semanticIndex.entryChunks }} chunks belonging to the {{ storyLab.semanticIndex.entryPages }} writing entries shown here.</p>

  <!--
    9. TOPIC FAMILIES AND TWO KINDS OF CONNECTION
    Automatic relationships: authored edges come from links between rendered
    entries; semantic edges come from passage pairs at the exploratory floor.
    Bespoke structure: radialGroupDefs assigns controlled-vocabulary topics to
    editorial families. An unassigned new topic falls into Other. Angle means
    family and radius means panel-specific cross-family connection count.
  -->
  <div class="kh-section-head kh-u-margin__top--1200">
    <h2 id="radial" class="kh-text-heading--2"><span class="kh-section-number">9.0</span>Topic families and two kinds of connection</h2>
    <span class="kh-meta">{{ storyLab.radial.counts.authored }} authored · {{ storyLab.radial.counts.semantic }} semantic · {{ storyLab.radial.counts.overlap }} both</span>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-content">
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> does the model connect subjects in the same way
    I deliberately connected them? In both diagrams, angle is an editorially
    assigned topic family. Radius is cross-family reach within that panel: more
    cross-family connections pull an entry closer to the centre.
    Curves bundle through an inner family anchor, borrowing the radial tree's
    visual grammar without claiming ancestry.<sup><a class="kh-lab-citation" href="#source-radial-family-trees" aria-label="Source 11: Radial Family Trees in Tableau">[11]</a></sup>
    The semantic panel deliberately replaces the public map's whole-entry
    averages and three-neighbour cap with passage-pair matches at
    {{ storyLab.radial.semanticMin }}, expanding {{ storyLab.radial.strictSemanticCount }}
    displayed pairs to {{ storyLab.radial.counts.semantic }}. Gold paths touch
    the AI family; heavier semantic paths have support in multiple distinct
    passages.</p>
  </div>
  <div id="kh-radial-comparison" class="kh-radial-comparison">
    <p class="kh-radial-radius-key kh-meta"><strong>Radius is cross-family reach.</strong> More connections pull a post towards the centre; zero-connected posts remain at the edge. Both panels use the same square-root 0–{{ storyLab.radial.crossDegreeMax }} scale so low counts remain distinguishable.</p>
    <div class="kh-radial-pair">
      {%- for panel in storyLab.radial.panels %}
      <figure class="kh-radial-panel" data-panel="{{ panel.key }}">
      <h3 class="kh-text-heading--3">{{ panel.label }}</h3>
      <svg class="kh-radial kh-radial--{{ panel.key }}" viewBox="0 0 {{ storyLab.radial.viewBox }} {{ storyLab.radial.viewBox }}" width="{{ storyLab.radial.viewBox }}" height="{{ storyLab.radial.viewBox }}" role="img" aria-labelledby="radial-title-{{ loop.index }} radial-desc-{{ loop.index }}">
        <title id="radial-title-{{ loop.index }}">{{ panel.label }} arranged by topic family and year</title>
        <desc id="radial-desc-{{ loop.index }}">One hundred entries are grouped around the circle by curated topic family. Entries with more cross-family connections in this panel sit nearer the centre. Curves show {{ panel.edges | length }} {{ panel.label | lower }}.</desc>
        <g class="kh-radial__rings" aria-hidden="true">
          {%- for ring in storyLab.radial.rings %}<circle cx="{{ storyLab.radial.centre }}" cy="{{ storyLab.radial.centre }}" r="{{ ring.radius }}"></circle>{%- endfor %}
          <line class="kh-radial__degree-axis" x1="{{ storyLab.radial.degreeAxis.start.x }}" y1="{{ storyLab.radial.degreeAxis.start.y }}" x2="{{ storyLab.radial.degreeAxis.end.x }}" y2="{{ storyLab.radial.degreeAxis.end.y }}"></line>
        </g>
        <g class="kh-radial__links">
          {%- for edge in panel.edges %}
          <g class="kh-radial__edge {% if edge.involvesAI %}is-ai {% endif %}{% if edge.crossGroup %}is-cross-group {% endif %}{% if edge.overlap %}is-overlap {% endif %}{% if panel.key == 'semantic' and edge.support >= 2 %}has-repeated-support {% endif %}{% if panel.key == 'semantic' and edge.support >= 4 %}has-broad-support{% endif %}" data-a="{{ edge.a }}" data-b="{{ edge.b }}" data-first-group="{{ edge.first.group }}" data-first-group-label="{{ edge.first.groupLabel }}" data-first-title="{{ edge.first.title }}" data-first-date="{{ edge.first.date }}" data-first-year="{{ edge.first.date | truncate(4, true, '') }}" data-second-group="{{ edge.second.group }}" data-second-group-label="{{ edge.second.groupLabel }}" data-second-title="{{ edge.second.title }}" data-second-date="{{ edge.second.date }}" data-second-year="{{ edge.second.date | truncate(4, true, '') }}" data-kind="{{ panel.key }}"{% if panel.key == 'semantic' %} data-score="{{ edge.score }}" data-matches="{{ edge.matches }}" data-support="{{ edge.support }}"{% else %} data-mentions="{{ edge.mentions }}"{% endif %}>
            <title>{{ edge.first.title }} ↔ {{ edge.second.title }}{% if panel.key == 'semantic' %}, {{ edge.matches }} matching passage pair{% if edge.matches != 1 %}s{% endif %} across at least {{ edge.support }} passage{% if edge.support != 1 %}s{% endif %} on each side, best similarity {{ edge.score }}{% else %}, {{ edge.mentions }} authored link{% if edge.mentions != 1 %}s{% endif %}{% endif %}</title>
            <path class="kh-radial__edge-line" d="{{ edge.path }}"></path>
            <path class="kh-radial__edge-hit" d="{{ edge.path }}"></path>
          </g>
          {%- endfor %}
        </g>
        <g class="kh-radial__nodes">
          {%- for node in panel.nodes %}
          <a class="kh-radial__node {% if node.isAI %}is-ai{% endif %}" href="{{ node.url }}" data-node="{{ node.index }}" data-group="{{ node.group }}" data-group-label="{{ node.groupLabel }}" data-title="{{ node.title }}" data-date="{{ node.date }}" data-year="{{ node.date | truncate(4, true, '') }}" data-topic="{{ node.topic }}" data-cross-links="{{ node.crossLinks }}" aria-describedby="kh-radial-detail">
            <title>{{ node.date }} — {{ node.title }} — {{ node.topic }} in {{ node.groupLabel }} — {{ node.crossLinks }} cross-family connection{% if node.crossLinks != 1 %}s{% endif %} in this panel</title>
            <circle class="kh-radial__node-hit" cx="{{ node.x }}" cy="{{ node.y }}" r="10"></circle>
            <circle class="kh-radial__node-mark" cx="{{ node.x }}" cy="{{ node.y }}" r="{% if node.isAI %}3.8{% else %}3{% endif %}"></circle>
          </a>
          {%- endfor %}
        </g>
        <g class="kh-radial__ring-labels" aria-hidden="true">
          {%- for ring in storyLab.radial.rings %}<text x="{{ ring.labelX }}" y="{{ ring.labelY }}">{{ ring.label }}</text>{%- endfor %}
        </g>
        <g class="kh-radial__labels">
          {%- for group in storyLab.radial.groups %}<g class="kh-radial__topic" data-group="{{ group.key }}"><title>{{ group.label }} — {{ group.entries }} entries</title><text x="{{ group.labelX }}" y="{{ group.labelY }}" text-anchor="{{ group.textAnchor }}">{{ group.label }}</text></g>{%- endfor %}
        </g>
      </svg>
      <figcaption class="kh-meta">{{ panel.edges | length }} pairs; {% if panel.key == 'authored' %}{{ storyLab.radial.counts.authoredCrossGroup }}{% else %}{{ storyLab.radial.counts.semanticCrossGroup }}{% endif %} cross a topic-family boundary.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      {%- endfor %}
    </div>
    <div class="kh-radial-timeline">
      <p class="kh-radial-timeline__heading"><span>Posts by year</span><span>{{ storyLab.radial.timeline.total }} entries</span></p>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 {{ storyLab.radial.timeline.width }} {{ storyLab.radial.timeline.height }}" width="{{ storyLab.radial.timeline.width }}" height="{{ storyLab.radial.timeline.height }}" role="img" aria-labelledby="radial-timeline-title radial-timeline-desc">
        <title id="radial-timeline-title">Posts by publication year</title>
        <desc id="radial-timeline-desc">A sparkline of the entries in the radial diagrams from 2013 to 2026. Highlighted years follow the active entry, connection or topic family above.</desc>
        <line class="kh-radial-timeline__baseline" x1="44" y1="{{ storyLab.radial.timeline.baseline }}" x2="1156" y2="{{ storyLab.radial.timeline.baseline }}"></line>
        <path class="kh-radial-timeline__trend" d="{{ storyLab.radial.timeline.path }}"></path>
        {%- for point in storyLab.radial.timeline.points %}
        <g class="kh-radial-year" data-year="{{ point.year }}">
          <title>{{ point.year }} — {{ point.count }} post{% if point.count != 1 %}s{% endif %}</title>
          <line class="kh-radial-year__stem" x1="{{ point.x }}" y1="{{ storyLab.radial.timeline.baseline }}" x2="{{ point.x }}" y2="{{ point.y }}"></line>
          <circle class="kh-radial-year__mark" cx="{{ point.x }}" cy="{{ point.y }}" r="4"></circle>
          <text class="kh-radial-year__count" x="{{ point.x }}" y="{{ point.y - 9 }}" text-anchor="middle">{{ point.count }}</text>
          <text class="kh-radial-year__label" x="{{ point.x }}" y="{{ storyLab.radial.timeline.baseline + 22 }}" text-anchor="middle">{{ point.year }}</text>
        </g>
        {%- endfor %}
      </svg>
    </div>
    <p id="kh-radial-detail" class="kh-radial-detail kh-meta" aria-live="polite">Hover a topic, entry or connection; keyboard users can focus an entry link.</p>
    <div class="kh-radial-tooltip" role="tooltip" aria-hidden="true">
      <div class="kh-radial-tooltip__entries">
        <div class="kh-radial-tooltip__entry" data-tooltip-entry="first"><span class="kh-radial-tooltip__label"></span><strong></strong></div>
        <div class="kh-radial-tooltip__entry" data-tooltip-entry="second"><span class="kh-radial-tooltip__label"></span><strong></strong></div>
      </div>
      <span class="kh-radial-tooltip__meta"></span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-content">
    <p>The contrast is itself a possible story: authored links cross topic-family
    boundaries much more often than the semantic map does. That may mean the
    writing makes explicit arguments between domains while the model mostly
    groups repeated vocabulary within them.</p>
  </div>

  <!--
    10. WHEN SUBJECTS ENTERED THE ARCHIVE
    Automatic: includes every primary topic used by at least two entries and
    orders topics by first appearance. Each trace is a trailing six-month count,
    normalized to that topic's own peak; dots mark actual publication months and
    the right label preserves the raw total. The six-month window and the 2013
    through 2026 time domain are currently fixed constants.
  -->
  <div class="kh-section-head kh-u-margin__top--1200">
    <h2 id="sparks" class="kh-text-heading--2"><span class="kh-section-number">10.0</span>When subjects entered the archive</h2>
    <span class="kh-meta">{{ storyLab.topicSparks.topics }} recurring topics · {{ storyLab.topicSparks.entries }} entries</span>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-content">
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> when did each subject first appear, and did it
    become a continuing practice, a short burst or a later return? Each line is
    a trailing {{ storyLab.topicSparks.windowMonths }}-month publication count,
    ordered by the topic's first appearance. Like Kevin Marks's
    <cite>The Joy of Sparks</cite>, the offset, overlapping lines turn small time
    series into a shared terrain.<sup><a class="kh-lab-citation" href="#source-joy-of-sparks" aria-label="Source 12: The Joy of Sparks">[12]</a></sup>
    Every topic is normalized to its own peak, so height compares timing and
    shape within a row, never volume between rows; the right-hand number is the
    actual entry total.</p>
  </div>
  <figure id="kh-topic-sparks" class="kh-topic-sparks">
    <div class="kh-map-scroll">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 {{ storyLab.topicSparks.width }} {{ storyLab.topicSparks.height }}" width="{{ storyLab.topicSparks.width }}" height="{{ storyLab.topicSparks.height }}" role="img" aria-labelledby="topic-sparks-title topic-sparks-desc">
        <title id="topic-sparks-title">Publication activity by recurring primary topic</title>
        <desc id="topic-sparks-desc">{{ storyLab.topicSparks.topics }} topic sparklines run from 2013 to 2026 and are ordered by first appearance. Each shows a trailing six-month count normalized to its own peak; exact post dates are marked with dots and actual totals appear at right.</desc>
        <g class="kh-topic-sparks__years" aria-hidden="true">
          {%- for year in storyLab.topicSparks.years %}
          <line x1="{{ year.x }}" y1="24" x2="{{ year.x }}" y2="{{ storyLab.topicSparks.height - 34 }}"></line>
          <text x="{{ year.x }}" y="{{ storyLab.topicSparks.height - 12 }}" text-anchor="middle">{{ year.year }}</text>
          {%- endfor %}
        </g>
        <g class="kh-topic-sparks__series-list">
          {%- for series in storyLab.topicSparks.series %}
          <a class="kh-topic-sparks__series" href="#sparks" data-topic="{{ series.topic }}" data-group="{{ series.groupLabel }}" data-total="{{ series.total }}" data-first="{{ series.firstDate }}" data-last="{{ series.lastDate }}" data-peak="{{ series.peak }}" data-peak-period="{{ series.peakPeriod }}" aria-describedby="kh-topic-sparks-detail">
            <title>{{ series.topic }} — {{ series.total }} entries from {{ series.firstDate }} to {{ series.lastDate }}; peak {{ series.peak }} in a trailing six-month window ending {{ series.peakPeriod }}</title>
            <path class="kh-topic-sparks__area" d="{{ series.area }}"></path>
            <path class="kh-topic-sparks__line" d="{{ series.line }}"></path>
            {%- for post in series.posts %}<circle class="kh-topic-sparks__post" cx="{{ post.x }}" cy="{{ post.y }}" r="2.8"><title>{{ post.date }} — {{ post.title }}</title></circle>{%- endfor %}
            <text class="kh-topic-sparks__label" x="{{ storyLab.topicSparks.plotLeft - 12 }}" y="{{ series.baseline + 4 }}" text-anchor="end">{{ series.topic }}</text>
            <text class="kh-topic-sparks__total" x="{{ storyLab.topicSparks.plotRight + 16 }}" y="{{ series.baseline + 4 }}">{{ series.total }}</text>
          </a>
          {%- endfor %}
        </g>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <figcaption id="kh-topic-sparks-detail" class="kh-meta" aria-live="polite">Hover a line or focus its label to read its span and peak. Topics with a single entry are omitted.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <details class="kh-map-details kh-u-margin__top--600">
    <summary>Limits shared by these experiments</summary>
    <div class="kh-content">
      <ul>
        <li>The corpus is uneven: {{ storyLab.careerBands[3].entries }} of {{ storyLab.counts.entries }} entries sit in the UNDRR-era band, with a public-writing gap in 2022–24.</li>
        <li>The public semantic map uses whole-entry similarity {{ semanticMap.edgeMin }} and at most three neighbours per entry. The radial semantic panel instead compares non-header passages at {{ storyLab.radial.semanticMin }} with no per-entry cap. Longer entries can produce more passage-pair matches, so line weight uses distinct passage support instead of the raw match count.</li>
        <li>Essays, digesting notes and impact stories are different genres. Similarity can mean recurrence, continuation or two accounts of the same work.</li>
        <li>{{ storyLab.counts.explicitLinks }} of the {{ storyLab.counts.links }} displayed pairs are linked directly in the source. The model often confirms an authored connection rather than discovering one.</li>
        <li>Career chapters are inferred from dates. They locate an entry in Ken's working life but do not prove an employer influenced it.</li>
        <li>The model proposes resemblance. Claims such as “this idea matured” or “I changed my mind” require close reading and editorial annotation.</li>
        <li>Outbound links are extracted from rendered article content. They include citations, tools, image credits and repeated links; they do not distinguish endorsement from criticism.</li>
        <li>Suffix groups describe URL endings, not ownership or trustworthiness. Career comparisons are especially fragile in the five linked entries from the DRS-era sample.</li>
        <li>The semantic index is the most recent full-build snapshot. Each passage vector also contains the entry title; the first, metadata-heavy chunk is excluded from passage comparisons.</li>
        <li>The radial families are editorial groupings, not clusters discovered by the model. Radius means cross-family connection count within that panel; angle means assigned family; neither diagram is a hierarchy or genealogy.</li>
        <li>The stacked topic sparklines omit one-off topics and normalize every recurring topic independently. They reveal timing and shape, but the line heights cannot be compared as publication volume.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </details>

  <section class="kh-lab-sources kh-u-margin__top--1200" aria-labelledby="sources">
    <h2 id="sources" class="kh-text-heading--2">Research precedents</h2>
    <div class="kh-content">
      <p>These experiments borrow visual grammar and storytelling questions,
      not the sources' palettes, typography or composition.</p>
      <ol>
        <li id="source-shape-of-song">Martin Wattenberg, <a href="https://www.bewitched.com/song.html"><cite>The Shape of Song</cite></a>: recurrence encoded as arcs along a sequence.</li>
        <li id="source-themeriver">Susan Havre, Elizabeth Hetzler and Lucy Nowell, <a href="https://www.pnnl.gov/publications/themeriver-visualizing-thematic-changes-document-collections"><cite>ThemeRiver</cite></a>: thematic change over time read alongside contextual events.</li>
        <li id="source-themedelta"><a href="https://sanghani.cs.vt.edu/research/publications/2017/themedelta-dynamic-segmentations-temporal-topic-models.html"><cite>ThemeDelta</cite></a>: topics splitting and merging over time.</li>
        <li id="source-on-broadway">Lev Manovich, <a href="https://manovich.net/index.php/art/on-broadway"><cite>On Broadway</cite></a>: multiple data layers aligned to a shared spine.</li>
        <li id="source-history-flow">Fernanda Viégas, Martin Wattenberg and Kushal Dave, <a href="https://research.ibm.com/publications/studying-cooperation-and-conflict-between-authors-with-history-flow-visualizations"><cite>History Flow</cite></a>: continuity, disappearance and reappearance across time.</li>
        <li id="source-bohemian-bookshelf"><a href="https://sheelaghcarpendale.ca/Research/BohemianBookshelf"><cite>The Bohemian Bookshelf</cite></a>: coordinated visual access points into one corpus.</li>
        <li id="source-dear-data">Giorgia Lupi and Stefanie Posavec, <a href="https://www.dear-data.com/theproject"><cite>Dear Data</cite></a>: personal documentary visualization with subjectivity visible.</li>
        <li id="source-writing-without-words">Stefanie Posavec, <a href="https://www.stefanieposavec.com/archive/writing-without-words"><cite>Writing Without Words</cite></a>: visualization as a close reading of significant writing.</li>
        <li id="source-text-visualization">Martin Wattenberg, <a href="https://www.bewitched.com/text-vis.html"><cite>Notes on text visualization</cite></a>: bringing words and phrases forward rather than relying only on aggregation.</li>
        <li id="source-pudding">The Pudding, <a href="https://pudding.cool/process/how-to-make-dope-shit-part-3/"><cite>How to make dope shit, part 3</cite></a>: exploration is raw material; a finished visualization still needs a story.</li>
        <li id="source-radial-family-trees">Brian Moore, <a href="https://domoorewithdata.com/2023/04/03/radial-family-trees-in-tableau/"><cite>Radial Family Trees in Tableau</cite></a>: radial position combines distance from the centre with position around the circle, while bundled line elements expose relationships within and between groups.</li>
        <li id="source-joy-of-sparks">Kevin Marks, <a href="https://kevinmarks.com/joyofsparks.html"><cite>The Joy of Sparks</cite></a>: vertically offset, filled activity sparklines make bursts and quiet periods legible as a layered terrain.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>
</section>
